00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:58.510
So first, what are data lakes?

00:00:58.510 --> 00:00:59.470
And why do they matter?

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:00.740
And it's a common phrase.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:03.400
So if you're familiar with
data lakes, I apologize.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:05.319
But I do want to
level set, just so we

00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:08.530
form a common understanding
of what we mean by data lakes.

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.870
So first and
foremost, data lakes

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:12.970
are a collection of raw data.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:14.890
They could be unstructured data.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:16.480
They could be structured data.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:18.430
They could be
semi-structured data.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:20.110
It's really just a
collection of data

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:21.790
that you've put
in a single store

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:23.410
for a variety of purposes.

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:25.990
So data lake is a good term.

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:29.290
You could look at it as a
giant container of data.

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:30.940
It's essentially a
collection of data

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:32.780
that you're going to
use for some purpose.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:35.200
And it is in some format.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.280
Data lakes typically
have a few key features.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.490
First, people often
put a lot of raw,

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:44.780
or unprocessed, or preprocessed
data in a data lake.

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:47.380
So it's very common for people
to take all of their data,

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:49.600
and as a first step, just
land it in a data lake

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.254
until they know what
they want to do with it.

00:01:52.254 --> 00:01:53.920
Second, it is very
common for data lakes

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:55.086
to have a mix of structures.

00:01:55.086 --> 00:01:58.510
So you may have a data lake that
has a lot of unstructured data

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:01.060
but is also sprinkled with
semi-structured or structured

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:03.970
data after it's been processed
or in the middle of being

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:05.550
processed.

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:08.590
Data lakes, third, are
not tied to processing.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:11.440
So data lakes are the
hallmark of the separation

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:12.760
of storage and compute.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:15.220
So when you store
data in a data lake,

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:18.010
it's not tied to any specific
processing technology.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:20.710
And most commonly, you'll
see that there's actually

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:22.810
multiple processing
technologies that are used

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:25.330
against one data lake itself.

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:27.400
That leads to data lakes
being multipurpose.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.830
Instead of having one
specialty function,

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:32.050
being really good at
one thing, data lakes

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:35.380
are a jack of all trades
in terms of a data store.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:37.930
They might not be the best
in one particular function.

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:40.360
But they serve a lot
of functions very well.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:44.730
Why build data lake?

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.950
Well, first,
scalability-- data lakes

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:48.970
are usually very scalable.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:50.550
So instead of
worrying about things

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:53.100
like how much
capacity do I need,

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.770
or how many disks
do I have to buy.

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:57.600
Instead, you just
think of a data lake

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.180
as almost an infinite store.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.519
Second, there's less
upfront work required.

00:03:02.519 --> 00:03:04.560
A key hallmark of data
lakes is that you can just

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.380
put data in without
thinking too much about it.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:10.410
You're not having to preprocess
the data or pre-apply

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:12.930
schemas to data,
not having to follow

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:15.080
any particular formatting rules.

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:16.890
Data lake, really
in an ideal sense,

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.740
should just be as simple as
putting the data in a store.

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:21.251
And you're done.

00:03:21.251 --> 00:03:23.250
You can use a variety of
tools with a data lake.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:26.630
So a good data lake will
have APIs built on top of it.

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:28.260
It will have third
party integrations

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:29.301
that you can use with it.

00:03:29.301 --> 00:03:30.690
A data lake should be connected.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:33.480
A data lake off by
itself or usable only

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:37.230
by a specific set of tools,
that's not a great data lake.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:39.360
That's just a really
isolated data silo

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:43.080
with maybe more data in
it or a variety of data.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:46.200
Because it is well connected,
infinitely scalable,

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.200
it should really be usable in
a variety of circumstances.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.540
That means organizational
circumstances.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:53.430
It means in terms
of applications.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:55.500
You might use the
data for analytics.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.420
You might use the data for ML.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:00.630
You might use the data just for
archive, and disaster recovery,

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:02.480
and retention.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:06.230
All of that is done in a
data lake with cost in mind.

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:08.960
So a real big reason
that a lot of people

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:12.680
are interested in data lakes
and build data lakes is cost.

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:16.160
Often, when you reason about
what data to keep or not keep,

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:17.880
you're trying to
think about, well,

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:19.130
how much does the data cost?

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:21.407
Is it worth the
data sitting around?

00:04:21.407 --> 00:04:22.490
Maybe I'm going to use it.

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:23.640
Maybe I won't.

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:26.090
Data lake, theoretically,
should remove as many

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:27.905
of those cost
concerns as possible.

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:33.120
So data lakes sound great.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:35.090
And a lot of people
talk about data lakes.

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:37.160
And there's a lot of
interest in data lakes.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:39.020
But there's a very
fair question of,

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:42.560
if data lakes are so great, why
isn't everybody building them?

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:45.680
Or why doesn't everybody
already have a data lake?

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:49.214
Well, I would postulate
there's a few reasons for that.

00:04:49.214 --> 00:04:50.630
One of the biggest
reasons I would

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:53.480
attribute to Conway's law.

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:56.060
Simply put, your
organizational structure

00:04:56.060 --> 00:05:00.890
will often define how you
organize, in some cases,

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:01.464
applications.

00:05:01.464 --> 00:05:02.880
But I'm going to
apply it to data.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.690
So essentially, your
organization chart

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:10.510
influences how you think
and break apart data.

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:13.930
So in this very simple
example, you have a set.

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:16.110
Let's say these are
different departments inside

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:17.446
of an organization.

00:05:17.446 --> 00:05:19.570
They're going to have their
different applications.

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:21.630
They're going to have their
different technologies.

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:23.550
They're going to have
their different needs--

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:26.370
all very legitimate,
all very valid.

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:28.210
That's their view of the world.

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:30.120
Unfortunately, that
siloed view of the world

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:31.770
is driving their
technology choices.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:33.990
It's driving what data
storage technology they're

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:36.030
thinking about, what
processing tools they're

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:38.850
using, how they think about
structured or unstructured

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:39.571
data.

00:05:39.571 --> 00:05:41.070
So without really
thinking about it,

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:43.950
the organizational chart is
actually almost clearly mapping

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:48.100
to the technology choices
that they're going to make.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:50.930
That makes data
integration really messy.

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:53.260
So now, you have all
of these technologies.

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:55.270
You have some data
that's unstructured.

00:05:55.270 --> 00:05:57.290
You have some data
that's structured.

00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:59.750
And it usually creates a
really big plumbing mess

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:02.320
that you then need yet
another group to go and fix.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:06.700
So you have three different
databases, two data warehouses,

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:08.140
raw data just
sitting everywhere.

00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:09.610
And now, you need
a massive effort

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:12.650
to go and try and tie
all of this together.

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:15.082
Now, I think it's fair to
say, most people don't wake up

00:06:15.082 --> 00:06:16.540
in the morning and
think, I'm going

00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:18.910
to go create just a giant
data mess for myself

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:20.514
because that sounds like fun.

00:06:20.514 --> 00:06:22.180
Instead, it's their
organizational chart

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:24.860
that's usually driving a lot
of decisions behind the scenes.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:28.450
And they just end up in a mess.

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:32.270
Data is also, as a result of
being organizationally driven,

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:33.630
it's not seen as an asset.

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:36.460
It's usually seen as a
cost in a lot of aspects.

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:39.400
So you're thinking about,
OK, what technology

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:41.050
do I need to store this data?

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:43.210
And then, data
becomes a cost center.

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:45.610
And people are buying
technology to store the data.

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:48.370
But at the end of
the day, you're

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:51.080
really just trying to think
about how can I lower my costs?

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:54.310
So how do I optimize costs
versus data being kept?

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:57.430
And instead of actually
deriving value from your data,

00:06:57.430 --> 00:07:00.515
you're just sitting there trying
to do accounting on your data.

00:07:00.515 --> 00:07:02.890
And I think, it's fair to say
that most people don't find

00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:05.020
a lot of value out of doing
accounting on their data,

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:06.811
unless they're dealing
with financial data.

00:07:06.811 --> 00:07:08.030
And that's their goal.

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:09.520
It also means that
any duplication

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:10.700
is highly inefficient.

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:13.450
So if you have two groups
that are storing data,

00:07:13.450 --> 00:07:16.630
when everything is a cost,
any duplication inside

00:07:16.630 --> 00:07:18.880
of two technologies that
groups may not even know

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:24.260
exist outside of that particular
group is extremely inefficient.

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:26.960
It also makes management
a complete headache.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:29.161
So you have all of these
different organizational

00:07:29.161 --> 00:07:29.660
groups.

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:32.720
They have all of the different
applications that are not only

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:34.736
storing but using data.

00:07:34.736 --> 00:07:36.860
They have all of these
different retention policies

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:38.771
they may want to apply to data.

00:07:38.771 --> 00:07:40.520
They have different
security requirements,

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:42.470
different retention
requirements.

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:45.020
So it usually leads
to programs where

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:46.940
you have to look at
an organizational view

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:49.100
of the world and
profile who has data?

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:50.390
How much data do they have?

00:07:50.390 --> 00:07:51.614
Where is that data located?

00:07:51.614 --> 00:07:53.030
What are their
retention policies?

00:07:53.030 --> 00:07:54.440
What is their security policies?

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:56.540
What are their schemas?

00:07:56.540 --> 00:07:59.990
Quite frankly, this usually
leads to large-scale efforts

00:07:59.990 --> 00:08:02.270
that take multiple years
to try and create a data

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:04.050
map across an organization.

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:05.852
Again, it might be
a worthwhile effort.

00:08:05.852 --> 00:08:07.310
And it's something
that people have

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:10.670
to do to make the world a
better place organizationally.

00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:13.770
But it's distracting from
what most people want to do,

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:15.710
which is just actually
use data and drive

00:08:15.710 --> 00:08:17.750
value from their data.

00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:19.820
A common solution
to this problem

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:21.182
is the drive to data warehouses.

00:08:21.182 --> 00:08:23.390
And I'll preface this with
saying data lakes and data

00:08:23.390 --> 00:08:25.040
warehouses co-exist.

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:28.869
So don't interpret the slides
saying data lakes and data

00:08:28.869 --> 00:08:30.410
warehouses are
diametrically opposed.

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:31.400
They actually co-exist.

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:33.890
And there's really good reasons
why we actually want both.

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:37.820
But a common knee jerk
reaction to the data messiness

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:40.130
that's driven by all of this
organizational complexity

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:41.870
is centralization.

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:44.990
And a very common
form of centralization

00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:47.250
is creating the large
scale-data warehouse.

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:49.440
And then, you take
all of these efforts,

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:52.160
and then you design schemas
that are cross-functional.

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:54.320
And you have committees
that try and figure

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:58.187
out what schemas is you need and
how you break everything apart.

00:08:58.187 --> 00:09:00.020
You may drive value
from the data warehouse.

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:02.964
But that also introduces its own
complexities in terms of cost,

00:09:02.964 --> 00:09:05.630
in terms of getting everybody to
agree on what schemas you want,

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:07.100
in terms of access.

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:11.315
So just driving to a data
warehouse is an end result.

00:09:11.315 --> 00:09:15.470
It makes sense in some
cases but not all cases.

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:21.730
So data lakes can simplify and
optimize for these problems.

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:24.190
Very simply, a data
lake can break down

00:09:24.190 --> 00:09:25.690
organizational walls.

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:28.530
So it seems very simple
because it is actually

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:29.740
a pretty simple concept.

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:32.567
Instead of focusing on
different data technologies

00:09:32.567 --> 00:09:34.400
because you have these
organizational silos,

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:37.660
and you have people thinking
in a box, with a common store,

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:40.690
with a common data lake-- if
you put data in that data lake--

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:42.270
it's much easier to think about.

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:45.460
It's much easier for teams
to see what other teams have.

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:47.050
It's much easier
to store that data.

00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:49.870
It's much easier to think
about security, much easier

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:51.420
to think about retention.

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:53.560
All because instead
of trying to silo data

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:55.730
and partition it across
different technologies,

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:57.700
different processing
tools, you're

00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:01.050
putting it in one data lake.

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:03.010
I mentioned that data
lakes and data warehouses

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:03.880
are complementary.

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:05.060
And that is very true.

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:09.370
So we'll talk about how data
lakes and data warehouses

00:10:09.370 --> 00:10:11.560
are complementary inside of GCP.

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:13.010
But they both have
their purpose.

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:16.760
A data lake is a great, single,
shared, common resource.

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:19.600
It's a great place to
store data of all types.

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:24.750
It's really, in many cases, jack
of all trades, master of none.

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:27.310
Data takes can be read
from many applications.

00:10:27.310 --> 00:10:30.649
Data lakes have a great
utility in storing data

00:10:30.649 --> 00:10:32.440
until you know what
you want to do with it,

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:35.480
or until you load it
into a subset of data

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:38.230
and often put it
into an application

00:10:38.230 --> 00:10:41.020
or a specific purpose.

00:10:41.020 --> 00:10:44.380
Now, data warehouses are
really good at what they do.

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:46.720
There are very good
reasons why you

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:49.960
would want to take a subset of
data, and apply schemas to it,

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:53.920
and have subsecond or very low
latency queriability on that

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:57.370
data, ensure that the data
is clean, is formatted,

00:10:57.370 --> 00:10:59.140
is consistent.

00:10:59.140 --> 00:11:01.210
Data warehouses are
very, very good at that.

00:11:01.210 --> 00:11:03.310
Data warehouses are
not very good at just

00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:05.650
storing lots and lots
of data because you

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:07.780
go through the process
of loading that data,

00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:10.760
normalizing that data, and
planting schemas for that data.

00:11:10.760 --> 00:11:12.730
Likewise, data lakes are
not great at offering

00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:13.960
subsecond queries.

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:17.140
Even with tools like Hiver,
and some cases Presto,

00:11:17.140 --> 00:11:19.840
you may get to lower
latency queries.

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:22.390
But they're not going to
generally match the semantics

00:11:22.390 --> 00:11:23.330
of data warehouses.

00:11:23.330 --> 00:11:25.870
So both of these tools
are very complementary

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:28.071
in a data-driven organization.

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:32.490
I want to talk
through how you would

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:35.557
build a data lake on GCP if
you were to get started today.

00:11:35.557 --> 00:11:37.140
And this is coming
from the assumption

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:40.380
that you just don't have
any data in GCP at all.

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:45.980
I mentioned a
hallmark of databases

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:49.280
that you have the
single store of data.

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:52.120
And in Google Cloud
Platform, cloud storage

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:56.340
is the hub or the central
spoke of a data lake.

00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:59.020
There's a lot of
semantics tied to cloud

00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:02.410
storage that make
it the central hub

00:12:02.410 --> 00:12:04.480
or spoke for your data lake.

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:07.000
First, you can use it
across many, many products.

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:09.310
cloud storage is well
connected and well integrated

00:12:09.310 --> 00:12:11.590
to many cloud platform products.

00:12:11.590 --> 00:12:13.757
You can optimize your
cost and availability

00:12:13.757 --> 00:12:16.090
based on different availability
tiers, different storage

00:12:16.090 --> 00:12:18.130
tiers inside of cloud storage.

00:12:18.130 --> 00:12:20.980
I did, in fact, count the number
of nines because I was curious.

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:24.180
And there's nine nines
after the decimal.

00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:27.100
There's very high
durability in cloud storage.

00:12:27.100 --> 00:12:28.930
You don't need to
think about how

00:12:28.930 --> 00:12:31.590
you're going to necessarily back
up your data or what happens.

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:33.910
It's incredibly durable.

00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:36.160
You can also apply centralized
management and security

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:37.240
policies.

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:39.160
So if you've not
use cloud storage,

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:40.900
before you can apply
policies on buckets.

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:42.820
You can apply
policies on objects.

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:48.250
You have a lot of flexibility to
specify what happens to items.

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:49.840
And inside of a
data lake, that's

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:51.798
nice because you can do
that in a central place

00:12:51.798 --> 00:12:53.890
instead of having to
figure out whichever

00:12:53.890 --> 00:12:57.760
number of technologies are
using to try and figure out then

00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:00.270
how to apply policies to them.

00:13:00.270 --> 00:13:03.450
Something that some people
know about that's not obvious

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:06.240
but one of the most interesting
features for cloud storage

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:07.796
is immediate consistency.

00:13:07.796 --> 00:13:09.420
And I'll talk about
this as it pertains

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:11.970
to the Spark and
Hadoop ecosystem,

00:13:11.970 --> 00:13:14.180
which I know quite well.

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:16.690
But immediate consistency
is actually really,

00:13:16.690 --> 00:13:18.180
really important in a data lake.

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:20.880
The best way to summarize media
consistency is when you put

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:23.790
something in the data lake--
in this case, cloud storage--

00:13:23.790 --> 00:13:26.390
it's immediately available
through a list operation

00:13:26.390 --> 00:13:27.720
in cloud storage.

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:30.930
This is really important if
you have multiple producers

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:34.050
and consumers that are
pointing at the same bucket.

00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:35.820
If somebody puts a
file there, and you

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:37.807
want to immediately
process that file,

00:13:37.807 --> 00:13:40.140
you want that file to be
immediately available in a list

00:13:40.140 --> 00:13:40.710
operation.

00:13:40.710 --> 00:13:42.330
Otherwise, somebody may miss it.

00:13:42.330 --> 00:13:45.130
Or worse, if one person
gets it in a list operation

00:13:45.130 --> 00:13:48.360
and another person does not get
it in a list operational, all

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:50.940
of a sudden, you
have discontinuity

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:53.470
between two different
users of your data.

00:13:53.470 --> 00:13:56.310
So the fact that cloud storage
has immediate list consistency

00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:59.960
is actually really,
really important.

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:02.000
In terms of thinking
about a data lake,

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:04.160
the different storage
classes in cloud storage

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:06.880
are very, very important
semantic for building a data

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:08.870
lake on GCP.

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:12.540
There's a couple of really
important points to take here.

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:16.790
First, there is a single
consistent storage API

00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:19.610
and availability across
all of these storage tiers.

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:23.720
So instead of having to try,
and reason, and think about,

00:14:23.720 --> 00:14:26.420
do I use regional
buckets or Coldline,

00:14:26.420 --> 00:14:29.630
and how long does it take to
get something out of Coldline?

00:14:29.630 --> 00:14:33.140
Or what different APIs do I
need to use to access Coldline

00:14:33.140 --> 00:14:34.550
versus Nearline?

00:14:34.550 --> 00:14:36.500
You don't have to think
about different APIs.

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:39.710
You don't have to think about
latency for getting things out

00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:40.370
of Coldline.

00:14:40.370 --> 00:14:43.850
You don't need to introduce
seconds, minutes, hours

00:14:43.850 --> 00:14:47.240
for getting data between
these availability tiers.

00:14:47.240 --> 00:14:50.180
The availability tiers also
allow you to pick and choose.

00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:52.070
Sometimes, there are
very valid reasons

00:14:52.070 --> 00:14:55.440
why you want things
available very, very quickly.

00:14:55.440 --> 00:15:03.110
There are a lot of times you may
want something that costs less,

00:15:03.110 --> 00:15:05.210
but you're going to
access less frequently.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:08.587
So in lot of cases, people
will take archived data

00:15:08.587 --> 00:15:11.170
that they don't expect to use,
and they'll put it in Coldline.

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:18.000
You can also apply policies
on buckets and on objects.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:19.600
And that's actually
really important.

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:22.500
So in this case, say you
have a bunch of things.

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:24.690
And you keep them in
a regional bucket.

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:27.600
And then after 30 days, you
want to put them in Nearline.

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:29.460
You keep them in
Nearline for 30 days,

00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:33.220
and then, you put them
in Coldline for a year.

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:35.380
This is really important
because this is actually

00:15:35.380 --> 00:15:38.740
very similar to how a
lot of organizations

00:15:38.740 --> 00:15:40.460
think about and use data.

00:15:40.460 --> 00:15:43.270
There's generally only a
subset of data, which they

00:15:43.270 --> 00:15:45.590
are using with any regularity.

00:15:45.590 --> 00:15:48.250
It's very uncommon for people
to use all of their data

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:49.750
all the time.

00:15:49.750 --> 00:15:53.470
It's very common for people to
access or work on their most

00:15:53.470 --> 00:15:55.450
recent data most frequently.

00:15:55.450 --> 00:15:57.560
And they still want access
to their older data.

00:15:57.560 --> 00:16:00.101
But they don't necessarily need
access all the time or access

00:16:00.101 --> 00:16:01.307
is very, very infrequent.

00:16:01.307 --> 00:16:02.890
And there's a lot
of reasons for that.

00:16:02.890 --> 00:16:06.050
The data that people
produce changes over time.

00:16:06.050 --> 00:16:09.546
The trustworthiness or
accuracy of the data

00:16:09.546 --> 00:16:10.420
may change over time.

00:16:10.420 --> 00:16:11.960
It doesn't mean you want
to get rid of the data.

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:13.626
It just means that
you don't necessarily

00:16:13.626 --> 00:16:14.920
need access to it all the time.

00:16:17.630 --> 00:16:22.440
Data lakes fit into a flow
in thinking about data.

00:16:22.440 --> 00:16:25.820
So when you ingest data,
it's very, very common

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:28.340
to ingest data into
that data lake.

00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:31.100
And then, trying to figure
out-- or maybe already

00:16:31.100 --> 00:16:34.130
know-- but that
data lake is there

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:38.730
until you take action on that
data in some way or some form.

00:16:38.730 --> 00:16:42.090
So for example, you may ship all
of your logs, all of your blob

00:16:42.090 --> 00:16:42.590
data.

00:16:42.590 --> 00:16:44.481
You may have images.

00:16:44.481 --> 00:16:45.980
Store all of that
in your data lake.

00:16:45.980 --> 00:16:47.810
In some cases, people
already know what

00:16:47.810 --> 00:16:49.101
they want to do with that data.

00:16:49.101 --> 00:16:51.050
And they just take a
subset of that data,

00:16:51.050 --> 00:16:54.680
and process it, and put it in
maybe a transactional database

00:16:54.680 --> 00:16:57.410
or some front of house system.

00:16:57.410 --> 00:17:00.170
In other cases, people may want
to keep data in that data lake,

00:17:00.170 --> 00:17:01.430
until they know what to do.

00:17:01.430 --> 00:17:02.930
And that's the power
of a data lake.

00:17:02.930 --> 00:17:06.290
It gives you that flexibility
to keep the data at rest.

00:17:06.290 --> 00:17:08.599
You may immediately
act on a subset of it

00:17:08.599 --> 00:17:11.060
and put it in a
particular place.

00:17:11.060 --> 00:17:13.040
And you only have to
act on a subset of it.

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:16.490
You don't have to keep the
whole data chunk in one piece,

00:17:16.490 --> 00:17:19.602
putting it in a more
specialized system.

00:17:19.602 --> 00:17:21.060
Or you can just
keep the data there

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:24.085
until you know what
you want to do with it.

00:17:24.085 --> 00:17:25.460
There's multiple
places where you

00:17:25.460 --> 00:17:29.390
may want to think about
loading and reading data inside

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:30.890
of Google Cloud Platform.

00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:33.170
There's a lot of
text on this slide.

00:17:33.170 --> 00:17:35.930
Thankfully, a lot of people are
taking pictures of the slide.

00:17:35.930 --> 00:17:37.790
All of this is also
documented online

00:17:37.790 --> 00:17:41.980
in a guide that I will post a
link to at the very end here.

00:17:41.980 --> 00:17:44.690
But the key point
I want to make here

00:17:44.690 --> 00:17:48.320
is GCS, as the center
of your data lake,

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:52.080
is great for just storing
large amounts of data.

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:54.140
There are other storage
products that you can

00:17:54.140 --> 00:17:57.760
use for more specific needs.

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:01.117
So application-based needs,
you may want data inside

00:18:01.117 --> 00:18:02.450
of an enterprise data warehouse.

00:18:02.450 --> 00:18:04.160
And you load it into BigQuery.

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:07.640
You may want to put data
inside of Cloud Spanner.

00:18:07.640 --> 00:18:09.020
But most likely,
you're not going

00:18:09.020 --> 00:18:11.810
to want to put all of your
data in Cloud Spanner.

00:18:11.810 --> 00:18:14.450
All of your data is not
appropriate for Big Table.

00:18:14.450 --> 00:18:17.750
So with cloud storage, you
can keep all of your data

00:18:17.750 --> 00:18:20.000
in a centralized data
lake and then use

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:24.070
each technology appropriately
for a very specific purpose.

00:18:24.070 --> 00:18:26.410
There's multiple ways that
you can fill cloud storage.

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:28.090
So hypothetically,
say you have a lot

00:18:28.090 --> 00:18:31.390
of data that's just existing
somewhere in the known

00:18:31.390 --> 00:18:33.000
universe.

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:34.960
There's batch ways you
can load that data.

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:37.840
So you can use gsutil which
ships as part of the Google

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:39.220
Cloud SDK.

00:18:39.220 --> 00:18:41.590
There's a Storage
Transfer Service.

00:18:41.590 --> 00:18:43.060
You can use
something like distcp

00:18:43.060 --> 00:18:45.130
if you're using the Spark
and Hadoop ecosystem.

00:18:45.130 --> 00:18:46.930
Actually that's
very, very common

00:18:46.930 --> 00:18:50.600
for people that are moving data
from HDFS to cloud storage.

00:18:50.600 --> 00:18:53.100
You can stream the data
using something like Pub/Sub

00:18:53.100 --> 00:18:54.430
and Cloud Dataflow.

00:18:54.430 --> 00:18:57.560
There's also a
transfer appliance.

00:18:57.560 --> 00:18:58.970
There's also a
number of partners

00:18:58.970 --> 00:19:02.270
who can help you move data
to cloud storage in both

00:19:02.270 --> 00:19:05.510
a one-time and
replicated way depending

00:19:05.510 --> 00:19:08.940
on whichever needs you have.

00:19:08.940 --> 00:19:11.700
I want to provide some
conceptual examples

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:13.530
before I invite Zulily
to stage to provide

00:19:13.530 --> 00:19:19.420
a very practical example of
how data lake on GCP is useful

00:19:19.420 --> 00:19:23.056
and how that works in
theory and in practice.

00:19:23.056 --> 00:19:25.890
A really good example
is replacing HDFS

00:19:25.890 --> 00:19:27.510
in a Spark and Hadoop cluster--

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:30.150
very common for people to
have Spark and Hadoop clusters

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:33.450
to store data and for them
to scale that cluster out

00:19:33.450 --> 00:19:37.290
because they need to
store more data in HDFS.

00:19:37.290 --> 00:19:39.360
As an example of how
cloud storage can

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:41.440
be a really good
data lake in GCP,

00:19:41.440 --> 00:19:44.190
there's a Hadoop compatible
file system connector,

00:19:44.190 --> 00:19:46.380
or there's a connector
that implements the Hadoop

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:49.890
compatible file system API
available for Cloud Dataproc--

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:51.840
which means that you
can just read and write

00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:55.510
to and from cloud storage
via Spark and Hadoop.

00:19:55.510 --> 00:19:58.150
And you don't have to
think about HDFS anymore.

00:19:58.150 --> 00:20:01.230
That also frees you from having
to think about replication.

00:20:01.230 --> 00:20:04.590
It frees you from having
to think about scalability.

00:20:04.590 --> 00:20:07.230
It also generally is
incredibly performant.

00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:09.870
So you'll actually often
see much better performance

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:11.850
from cloud storage
with Cloud Dataproc

00:20:11.850 --> 00:20:15.272
than reading and writing even to
something like persistent disk.

00:20:15.272 --> 00:20:17.000
It doesn't necessarily
mean that you

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:21.350
would want to use cloud storage
for everything in the sun.

00:20:21.350 --> 00:20:23.660
In some cases,
temporary IO may be

00:20:23.660 --> 00:20:26.360
more appropriate on local SSD.

00:20:26.360 --> 00:20:30.020
It may mean that you want to
parallelize your workloads

00:20:30.020 --> 00:20:33.290
and avoid doing things
like massive renames.

00:20:33.290 --> 00:20:36.190
But in general, the nice thing
about having cloud storage

00:20:36.190 --> 00:20:38.180
as a data lake is you're
no longer thinking

00:20:38.180 --> 00:20:41.990
about the biggest pain, which
is that underlying file system

00:20:41.990 --> 00:20:45.390
and how you actually manage that
file system to store your data.

00:20:45.390 --> 00:20:46.500
I mentioned consistency.

00:20:46.500 --> 00:20:49.309
This is a good example of
why consistency is important.

00:20:49.309 --> 00:20:50.850
If you have multiple
Spark and Hadoop

00:20:50.850 --> 00:20:52.391
clusters that are
reading and writing

00:20:52.391 --> 00:20:54.120
from one bucket
at the same time,

00:20:54.120 --> 00:20:56.220
they all need to be in
sync in terms of what

00:20:56.220 --> 00:20:57.780
they see in that bucket.

00:20:57.780 --> 00:21:00.850
If they're out of sync, you're
going to get different results.

00:21:00.850 --> 00:21:03.480
So you're just dropping a
new file in every second.

00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:04.980
And all you're
doing in a Spark job

00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:06.450
is counting the number of files.

00:21:06.450 --> 00:21:07.944
Something that
simple, if you don't

00:21:07.944 --> 00:21:09.360
have immediate
consistency, you're

00:21:09.360 --> 00:21:12.760
going to get different
results very, very quickly.

00:21:12.760 --> 00:21:14.874
So consistency is a
really, really big deal

00:21:14.874 --> 00:21:15.540
for a data lake.

00:21:15.540 --> 00:21:16.998
And it's also a
really good example

00:21:16.998 --> 00:21:20.351
of why cloud storage is a
very, very good data lake.

00:21:20.351 --> 00:21:23.410
I mentioned segmenting
data by classes.

00:21:23.410 --> 00:21:27.330
So people generally don't
access all of the data at once.

00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:30.130
It's usually a very, very
small portion of their data,

00:21:30.130 --> 00:21:32.520
which means that in
a traditional world--

00:21:32.520 --> 00:21:34.040
in this case, HDFS--

00:21:34.040 --> 00:21:36.677
they're paying one cost
for all of their data

00:21:36.677 --> 00:21:38.760
even though they're only
accessing a small portion

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:40.500
of their data frequently.

00:21:40.500 --> 00:21:42.330
With different
storage classes, you

00:21:42.330 --> 00:21:44.760
can keep that
frequently-accessed data

00:21:44.760 --> 00:21:47.752
in something like a
regional bucket and then

00:21:47.752 --> 00:21:49.710
start segmenting your
data to different classes

00:21:49.710 --> 00:21:51.720
depending on how
frequently you think

00:21:51.720 --> 00:21:55.560
or you just know you
need to access that data.

00:21:55.560 --> 00:21:57.310
You also have a choice
of processing tools

00:21:57.310 --> 00:21:58.580
with cloud storage.

00:21:58.580 --> 00:22:01.220
So in this case, if you
have data in cloud storage,

00:22:01.220 --> 00:22:03.460
you can read that data
from Cloud Dataproc.

00:22:03.460 --> 00:22:05.380
You can read it from Dataflow.

00:22:05.380 --> 00:22:08.980
You can do Federated queries
on that data from BigQuery.

00:22:08.980 --> 00:22:12.130
You can analyze that
data with Cloud Dataprep.

00:22:12.130 --> 00:22:15.370
In this example, say you analyze
the data in Cloud Dataprep,

00:22:15.370 --> 00:22:18.300
and then, you load a subset
of that data into BigQuery--

00:22:18.300 --> 00:22:20.920
a very good example of
a data lake in action.

00:22:20.920 --> 00:22:23.440
Very likely, all of the
data that you would analyze

00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:25.180
with something like
Dataprep, you're

00:22:25.180 --> 00:22:27.310
not going to just dump
into BigQuery because say,

00:22:27.310 --> 00:22:29.610
you remove 15% of data.

00:22:29.610 --> 00:22:31.990
That's just not what you
need to keep in BigQuery.

00:22:31.990 --> 00:22:33.010
In all likelihood,
you don't want

00:22:33.010 --> 00:22:35.259
to immediately just destroy
that data, which gives you

00:22:35.259 --> 00:22:37.720
a very nice separation
between the appropriateness

00:22:37.720 --> 00:22:39.730
of BigQueries in
Enterprise Data Warehouse

00:22:39.730 --> 00:22:42.420
and cloud storage
as a data lake.

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:45.770
You also have the flexibility
of file formats in cloud storage

00:22:45.770 --> 00:22:48.240
and in general with data lakes.

00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:50.220
So in this case, say
you have data that's

00:22:50.220 --> 00:22:52.575
Parquet, and Avro, and ORC.

00:22:52.575 --> 00:22:54.900
You don't have to think
about different storage

00:22:54.900 --> 00:22:57.450
mediums for each type
of these file formats.

00:22:57.450 --> 00:22:58.950
Within even the
same folder, you can

00:22:58.950 --> 00:23:02.250
have files of all these
file formats coexist.

00:23:02.250 --> 00:23:04.820
As long as the consumer
knows what to expect

00:23:04.820 --> 00:23:08.190
or can introspect the files and
deal with them appropriately,

00:23:08.190 --> 00:23:09.330
you're good to go.

00:23:09.330 --> 00:23:11.370
You don't have to
configure your data lake

00:23:11.370 --> 00:23:15.010
and manage based
on the file format.

00:23:15.010 --> 00:23:16.960
There's a lot of
boxes on this line.

00:23:16.960 --> 00:23:19.450
The important takeaway
is cloud storage

00:23:19.450 --> 00:23:23.290
can be the central spoke
of your data platform

00:23:23.290 --> 00:23:25.250
as a data lake on GCP.

00:23:25.250 --> 00:23:27.370
So for example, you
could read data and batch

00:23:27.370 --> 00:23:29.080
from Dataflow or Dataproc.

00:23:29.080 --> 00:23:32.920
You can stream data from
Pub/Sub, from Dataflow.

00:23:32.920 --> 00:23:36.100
You can refine that
data, put it in something

00:23:36.100 --> 00:23:38.080
like Spanner, BigQuery.

00:23:38.080 --> 00:23:41.320
But at the Hub, all
of your just data--

00:23:41.320 --> 00:23:43.810
either that's been
processed or raw data--

00:23:43.810 --> 00:23:45.065
is inside of cloud storage.

00:23:45.065 --> 00:23:47.630
It has your data lake.

00:23:47.630 --> 00:23:50.200
With that, I want to invite
our friends from Zulily

00:23:50.200 --> 00:23:53.050
to stage to walk through
a practical example of how

00:23:53.050 --> 00:23:56.640
they built a data lake on GCP.

00:23:56.640 --> 00:24:00.420
GAURAV TANDON: I'm happy to be
here and share our experiences

00:24:00.420 --> 00:24:04.950
on board the GCP, the Google
Cloud Platform, 40 years ago,

00:24:04.950 --> 00:24:09.570
and specifically talk about
what Google Cloud Storage meant

00:24:09.570 --> 00:24:13.760
for us and did for us among
the other data products

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:15.330
that Google has to offer.

00:24:18.260 --> 00:24:20.980
So this presentation
is really talking

00:24:20.980 --> 00:24:23.230
about analytics, and
machine learning,

00:24:23.230 --> 00:24:25.150
and how cloud
storage can actually

00:24:25.150 --> 00:24:29.710
be a repository for
doing all of that.

00:24:29.710 --> 00:24:33.070
A little bit about myself.

00:24:33.070 --> 00:24:37.850
I'm director of machine
learning data science at Zulily.

00:24:37.850 --> 00:24:41.320
It's a loosely defined role.

00:24:41.320 --> 00:24:44.440
Machine learning data science
can mean very different things

00:24:44.440 --> 00:24:46.450
at different organizations.

00:24:46.450 --> 00:24:49.210
But at Zulily,
what this means is,

00:24:49.210 --> 00:24:52.900
I work with multiple teams
of data scientists, machine

00:24:52.900 --> 00:24:55.240
learning engineers,
and data engineers

00:24:55.240 --> 00:25:00.130
to drive experiences that engage
our customers-- whether they

00:25:00.130 --> 00:25:02.950
are external customers
or internal customers

00:25:02.950 --> 00:25:07.230
within the retail enterprise.

00:25:07.230 --> 00:25:09.520
So what is Zulily?

00:25:09.520 --> 00:25:15.000
Zulily is growing and
thriving in its own category

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:16.170
of e-commerce.

00:25:16.170 --> 00:25:20.670
And that's making shopping,
entertaining, and fun.

00:25:20.670 --> 00:25:23.490
We present a third
way to shop, which

00:25:23.490 --> 00:25:27.690
is discovery-based shopping,
unlike brick and mortar

00:25:27.690 --> 00:25:31.110
retailers and traditional
online retailers, which

00:25:31.110 --> 00:25:35.280
are intent-based,
directed source-driven.

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:39.450
Zulily is more about a browse
and discover experience.

00:25:39.450 --> 00:25:43.010
So it's a commeditized search.

00:25:43.010 --> 00:25:45.660
If you want cat food, you
go search for cat food.

00:25:45.660 --> 00:25:47.180
You'll find a bunch of options.

00:25:47.180 --> 00:25:51.060
You pick the one you like,
most relevant to your needs,

00:25:51.060 --> 00:25:53.440
and you go buy something.

00:25:53.440 --> 00:25:55.530
Zulily is not that.

00:25:55.530 --> 00:25:58.890
You think about Zulily as
a newspaper or magazine.

00:25:58.890 --> 00:26:03.210
There is a new
edition every day.

00:26:03.210 --> 00:26:05.860
And the goal is, we want
to drive engagement.

00:26:05.860 --> 00:26:08.650
We want our customers
to be curious about what

00:26:08.650 --> 00:26:11.820
do we have on the site
today and come back

00:26:11.820 --> 00:26:14.820
to see what's new and fresh.

00:26:14.820 --> 00:26:16.350
So how do we do that?

00:26:16.350 --> 00:26:21.270
We launch over 9,000
products every single day

00:26:21.270 --> 00:26:24.660
through 100 plus sales
events on our website.

00:26:24.660 --> 00:26:29.740
We work with around 15,000
brands to make this happen.

00:26:29.740 --> 00:26:32.230
So it's an interesting
business model.

00:26:32.230 --> 00:26:36.690
But what it requires is the
agility and the nimbleness

00:26:36.690 --> 00:26:39.270
across the board because
we are really revamping,

00:26:39.270 --> 00:26:42.600
relaunching that same
site over and over again

00:26:42.600 --> 00:26:44.880
every single day.

00:26:44.880 --> 00:26:47.280
And what it also
means is, we have

00:26:47.280 --> 00:26:51.780
to make data accessible
to all of our partners

00:26:51.780 --> 00:26:54.660
within the organization,
whether they are merchandisers

00:26:54.660 --> 00:26:58.020
we're working with, the external
vendors to get their products

00:26:58.020 --> 00:26:59.790
onto the Zulily platform.

00:26:59.790 --> 00:27:01.700
Or they are
marketing specialists

00:27:01.700 --> 00:27:04.170
who are trying to figure out
what kind of ad campaigns

00:27:04.170 --> 00:27:07.800
to launch the same day, the
next day, or the next week.

00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:09.580
Data is key.

00:27:09.580 --> 00:27:12.990
We also have three fulfillment
centers in the United States.

00:27:12.990 --> 00:27:17.460
And so we want all
of our associates

00:27:17.460 --> 00:27:19.620
in the fulfillment
center to have access

00:27:19.620 --> 00:27:23.160
to data near real-time
and really drive

00:27:23.160 --> 00:27:27.340
their decision making
using that data.

00:27:27.340 --> 00:27:33.240
So that was really the goal and
how we are as an organization.

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:36.030
So where is Zulily today?

00:27:36.030 --> 00:27:37.630
By the way, we launched in 2010.

00:27:37.630 --> 00:27:41.340
So we are a relatively
young company, 8-year-old.

00:27:41.340 --> 00:27:44.340
But right now, 70% of
our orders are actually

00:27:44.340 --> 00:27:46.720
placed through mobile devices.

00:27:46.720 --> 00:27:50.190
And 91% of our
orders are actually

00:27:50.190 --> 00:27:52.290
coming through repeat customers.

00:27:52.290 --> 00:27:53.340
And that is the key.

00:27:53.340 --> 00:27:56.082
And that is really the
focus of this talk.

00:27:56.082 --> 00:27:58.530
It didn't happen overnight.

00:27:58.530 --> 00:28:00.730
It's a process that
we went through.

00:28:00.730 --> 00:28:04.200
So what was our journey
where we ended up here?

00:28:04.200 --> 00:28:09.970
And how did Google help in that
journey to make this happen?

00:28:09.970 --> 00:28:15.780
We lost the presentation
on that site.

00:28:15.780 --> 00:28:16.280
OK.

00:28:22.550 --> 00:28:25.550
So four years ago, the
business was growing.

00:28:25.550 --> 00:28:29.060
Our product categories
were expanding.

00:28:29.060 --> 00:28:31.250
The customer base was
growing significantly.

00:28:31.250 --> 00:28:34.300
And that is a time
where the company,

00:28:34.300 --> 00:28:38.690
we recognized that the
structure we had in place

00:28:38.690 --> 00:28:43.820
did not meet the needs for
our future growth potential.

00:28:43.820 --> 00:28:46.520
That's where we started looking
at what are our options.

00:28:46.520 --> 00:28:48.470
We started looking
at the big data.

00:28:48.470 --> 00:28:50.480
But at the core
of that, we really

00:28:50.480 --> 00:28:54.870
wanted to make our
own big data platform.

00:28:54.870 --> 00:28:59.730
We are a tech company at heart.

00:28:59.730 --> 00:29:03.110
We have a bias to build, a
very strong bias to build.

00:29:03.110 --> 00:29:06.250
And from that perspective, we
went back to the drawing board

00:29:06.250 --> 00:29:09.860
and said, hey, we want to
build this big data platform

00:29:09.860 --> 00:29:12.600
to democratize data
to all of our partners

00:29:12.600 --> 00:29:15.080
internally and also
improve our customer

00:29:15.080 --> 00:29:19.230
experience to those who are
coming to our site every day.

00:29:19.230 --> 00:29:22.040
So there were key
components there.

00:29:22.040 --> 00:29:26.180
Back in the day, we had silos of
data, unstructured data coming

00:29:26.180 --> 00:29:27.390
through clickstream.

00:29:27.390 --> 00:29:29.390
Who's clicking on what page?

00:29:29.390 --> 00:29:30.830
What products are they viewing?

00:29:30.830 --> 00:29:32.400
Which one they added to cart?

00:29:32.400 --> 00:29:34.290
What did they purchase?

00:29:34.290 --> 00:29:36.090
So all of that
data is coming in.

00:29:36.090 --> 00:29:37.460
It's all unstructured.

00:29:37.460 --> 00:29:41.240
On the other hand, we had
transactional data stores.

00:29:41.240 --> 00:29:45.920
Every application, whether
it's the products customers

00:29:45.920 --> 00:29:50.390
are buying to our
marketing campaigns.

00:29:50.390 --> 00:29:54.590
All of that data lived in
traditional SQL-like data

00:29:54.590 --> 00:29:56.040
stores.

00:29:56.040 --> 00:29:58.610
So we wanted to get
everything together.

00:29:58.610 --> 00:30:01.670
Correlating something
from a user interaction

00:30:01.670 --> 00:30:04.160
and unstructured data
in the clickstream,

00:30:04.160 --> 00:30:06.530
to something on our
transactional DB

00:30:06.530 --> 00:30:09.080
was a hard thing at that time.

00:30:09.080 --> 00:30:10.400
So it was clear to us.

00:30:10.400 --> 00:30:13.760
We had to get the data
together in a single place

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:16.010
and then figure out
a mechanism to make

00:30:16.010 --> 00:30:20.150
it available to everyone
and in a timely fashion.

00:30:20.150 --> 00:30:21.610
Scalability was important.

00:30:21.610 --> 00:30:26.270
Reliability, durability,
flexibility-- our business

00:30:26.270 --> 00:30:27.440
is really flexible.

00:30:27.440 --> 00:30:31.910
So we wanted to have factor
in all the flexibility upfront

00:30:31.910 --> 00:30:35.210
before we proceeded
with anything.

00:30:35.210 --> 00:30:39.210
We had workloads with
varying requirements.

00:30:39.210 --> 00:30:41.480
Some of them would
be batch processes

00:30:41.480 --> 00:30:44.260
that we would run once a day.

00:30:44.260 --> 00:30:47.820
Some were every 30 minutes.

00:30:47.820 --> 00:30:52.120
But there were also a desire to
get the data in near real time.

00:30:52.120 --> 00:30:56.240
So there was definitely a
real time component of that.

00:30:56.240 --> 00:30:59.370
And we wanted part
of all of that data,

00:30:59.370 --> 00:31:03.150
which in the first place, we
didn't know what data we needed

00:31:03.150 --> 00:31:05.250
and how we would use that data.

00:31:05.250 --> 00:31:07.380
And that's where the
data lake came in,

00:31:07.380 --> 00:31:09.360
the centralized data storage.

00:31:09.360 --> 00:31:12.880
Can we just put all of the
data, pocket somewhere,

00:31:12.880 --> 00:31:15.180
and over time, we'll
figure out what's

00:31:15.180 --> 00:31:17.700
the best use of that data.

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:21.300
And as we determine
what that user

00:31:21.300 --> 00:31:24.840
scenario, the key scenario, is
that we're trying to target,

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:28.020
that's when we will do all
of the transformations.

00:31:28.020 --> 00:31:30.390
And magic will
happen on this data.

00:31:30.390 --> 00:31:34.170
And we will make it available
in our big data warehouse, which

00:31:34.170 --> 00:31:37.200
will be available to the
rest of the organization.

00:31:37.200 --> 00:31:42.540
And they will run their business
from this big data warehouse.

00:31:42.540 --> 00:31:47.080
Data access and
visualization is key.

00:31:47.080 --> 00:31:48.720
We do a lot of reporting.

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:51.060
Like I mentioned, our
fulfillment centers

00:31:51.060 --> 00:31:54.030
need to know what's the
status of all the orders,

00:31:54.030 --> 00:31:55.650
how is shipping happening.

00:31:55.650 --> 00:31:59.850
Our marketers need to know
how their ad campaigns are

00:31:59.850 --> 00:32:02.050
performing over the
course of the day.

00:32:02.050 --> 00:32:04.890
Do we need to course
correct something based

00:32:04.890 --> 00:32:06.270
on the data we are seeing?

00:32:06.270 --> 00:32:10.020
So there was a strong desire
to get data as soon as possible

00:32:10.020 --> 00:32:14.274
and also be able to visualize
it for easier consumption.

00:32:17.120 --> 00:32:19.490
And the core data services,
which we're really

00:32:19.490 --> 00:32:22.050
making that data
available to everyone,

00:32:22.050 --> 00:32:25.340
whether it's a non-tech
partner in different parts

00:32:25.340 --> 00:32:28.340
of the organization, or
it is for consumption

00:32:28.340 --> 00:32:30.890
within the technology
organization itself.

00:32:35.410 --> 00:32:39.440
So our journey was more around
the centralized big data

00:32:39.440 --> 00:32:39.940
storage.

00:32:39.940 --> 00:32:43.120
We evaluated Google
Cloud Storage.

00:32:43.120 --> 00:32:46.300
We really liked that it
supported all of our needs

00:32:46.300 --> 00:32:49.420
from the structure to
the unstructured data.

00:32:49.420 --> 00:32:52.390
And like I said, at the
time, we were not even clear.

00:32:52.390 --> 00:32:55.090
We had a few course scenarios
we wanted to address.

00:32:55.090 --> 00:32:57.520
But we wanted to
future-proof ourselves.

00:32:57.520 --> 00:33:00.480
So it really fed the deeds.

00:33:00.480 --> 00:33:06.580
For the big data
warehouse, it was BigQuery.

00:33:06.580 --> 00:33:11.140
And again there, we have
over 100-plus disparate data

00:33:11.140 --> 00:33:12.700
sources that we are
actually pulling

00:33:12.700 --> 00:33:16.390
the data from at
different time intervals.

00:33:16.390 --> 00:33:17.980
Some of them are near real time.

00:33:17.980 --> 00:33:23.080
Some of them running every
hour or every day cadence.

00:33:23.080 --> 00:33:25.240
So we've had to build
some tooling around that.

00:33:25.240 --> 00:33:28.060
We built some
capability internally

00:33:28.060 --> 00:33:31.150
that would actually
synchronize the data

00:33:31.150 --> 00:33:33.880
from the disparate sources,
whether it's a SQL data

00:33:33.880 --> 00:33:37.330
store, non-sequel data
store, or just gathering

00:33:37.330 --> 00:33:39.900
all of the real-time
clickstream and make

00:33:39.900 --> 00:33:42.580
it available in the centralized
data storage, that's

00:33:42.580 --> 00:33:45.040
GCS, Google Cloud Storage.

00:33:45.040 --> 00:33:48.370
And then, we actually used
a lot of static clusters

00:33:48.370 --> 00:33:51.880
in the beginning and then
moved on to Google Dataproc

00:33:51.880 --> 00:33:55.990
to actually solve for some
of the core scenarios that

00:33:55.990 --> 00:33:58.790
were critical for the
rest of the organization.

00:33:58.790 --> 00:34:01.210
So what are the key data
sets that we wanted to make

00:34:01.210 --> 00:34:03.730
available to the
rest of the company

00:34:03.730 --> 00:34:07.580
that they can actually drive
their day-to-day business from?

00:34:07.580 --> 00:34:12.199
So we used Spark and Hadoop to
do all of the batch processing.

00:34:12.199 --> 00:34:14.409
In some cases, we
used Spark Streaming

00:34:14.409 --> 00:34:16.989
for real-time processing.

00:34:16.989 --> 00:34:20.850
And we made that data
available in BigQuery.

00:34:20.850 --> 00:34:25.270
And BigQuery was great because
the barrier to entry is low.

00:34:25.270 --> 00:34:28.570
It's not just limited to
data scientists and analysts.

00:34:28.570 --> 00:34:31.540
It's a very easy
SQL-like interface

00:34:31.540 --> 00:34:36.280
that I think half of our company
actually, or more than half

00:34:36.280 --> 00:34:38.590
the company actually, uses it.

00:34:38.590 --> 00:34:42.070
It's interesting because our
PR team just told me, hey,

00:34:42.070 --> 00:34:43.719
I went into BigQuery
the other day.

00:34:43.719 --> 00:34:44.750
And I got this data.

00:34:44.750 --> 00:34:47.320
So that was pretty
cool to see how

00:34:47.320 --> 00:34:51.310
people outside of technology
are embracing it and being

00:34:51.310 --> 00:34:54.962
able to leverage, to run
their decision making,

00:34:54.962 --> 00:34:55.795
to run the business.

00:35:00.170 --> 00:35:02.000
So that's really what
I was talking about,

00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:04.460
which is we wanted
to make the data

00:35:04.460 --> 00:35:06.890
available as a
self-service capability

00:35:06.890 --> 00:35:08.800
to the rest of the business.

00:35:08.800 --> 00:35:10.350
BigQuery is what we used.

00:35:10.350 --> 00:35:14.630
But really, it was the data
from Google Cloud Storage

00:35:14.630 --> 00:35:17.960
and manipulating,
transforming that data,

00:35:17.960 --> 00:35:20.750
joining multiple
data sets together

00:35:20.750 --> 00:35:23.780
to actually make something
meaningful and compelling

00:35:23.780 --> 00:35:26.180
for our business
stakeholders to consume.

00:35:26.180 --> 00:35:28.670
That is what is
available in BigQuery.

00:35:28.670 --> 00:35:30.800
And they can do all of
the reporting that's

00:35:30.800 --> 00:35:32.330
boarding on top of that.

00:35:32.330 --> 00:35:35.660
They can actually get
any intraday statistics

00:35:35.660 --> 00:35:39.624
that they need to answer some
of the business questions.

00:35:43.500 --> 00:35:45.560
The other thing which
was really interesting

00:35:45.560 --> 00:35:50.630
and was an outcome of this, we
have a lot of in-built tools

00:35:50.630 --> 00:35:51.570
and applications.

00:35:51.570 --> 00:35:54.570
I mentioned, we have a huge
merchandising organization

00:35:54.570 --> 00:35:59.750
that's getting all of our
vendors on board the platform.

00:35:59.750 --> 00:36:04.020
What we want to do is,
over the course of the day,

00:36:04.020 --> 00:36:06.530
as the products are
launched on the site,

00:36:06.530 --> 00:36:11.270
can we provide some insights
to our merchandisers

00:36:11.270 --> 00:36:13.520
and our external vendors on--

00:36:13.520 --> 00:36:15.380
1, how their
products are actually

00:36:15.380 --> 00:36:16.910
performing on the site?

00:36:16.910 --> 00:36:18.600
How much demand are
they generating?

00:36:18.600 --> 00:36:20.930
What does conversion look like?

00:36:20.930 --> 00:36:24.320
And really give them the
data and the insights

00:36:24.320 --> 00:36:26.730
they need to take action.

00:36:26.730 --> 00:36:30.350
And so we built some
applications around that.

00:36:30.350 --> 00:36:33.830
This is an example of our
merchandising dashboard.

00:36:33.830 --> 00:36:36.290
It's an application
which is used by over 500

00:36:36.290 --> 00:36:37.970
people within the company.

00:36:37.970 --> 00:36:42.260
But really, this is driving
them to look at the data,

00:36:42.260 --> 00:36:45.800
and consume the data
in an easy fashion,

00:36:45.800 --> 00:36:49.610
and then have something
that's actionable as a result

00:36:49.610 --> 00:36:53.730
of looking at this data and
the visualization itself.

00:36:53.730 --> 00:36:56.450
So once we rolled
this application out,

00:36:56.450 --> 00:37:01.640
we really saw there was high
engagement in our organization.

00:37:01.640 --> 00:37:07.640
And they really started to pick
up on big data and the Google

00:37:07.640 --> 00:37:08.580
Cloud Platform itself.

00:37:11.910 --> 00:37:15.080
The other thing I
wanted to stress on

00:37:15.080 --> 00:37:20.310
is, we have a dynamic
business model.

00:37:20.310 --> 00:37:22.990
Our catalog is
changing every day.

00:37:22.990 --> 00:37:27.040
We want to get people to come
back to our site every day.

00:37:27.040 --> 00:37:29.720
So we do that with
personalization.

00:37:29.720 --> 00:37:34.120
It's a one-to-one
hyper-personalized experience

00:37:34.120 --> 00:37:36.260
for every customer.

00:37:36.260 --> 00:37:38.290
So in that sense,
we are actually

00:37:38.290 --> 00:37:43.180
serving millions of versions
of our site every day.

00:37:43.180 --> 00:37:46.480
And we do it with
the power of the data

00:37:46.480 --> 00:37:49.870
that we are gathering from all
of the disparate data sources,

00:37:49.870 --> 00:37:53.500
trying to understand, what
do our customers like?

00:37:53.500 --> 00:37:55.480
What are their preferences?

00:37:55.480 --> 00:37:58.480
And really, hear from
them and learn from them.

00:37:58.480 --> 00:38:01.210
And then, take those
learnings to drive

00:38:01.210 --> 00:38:02.920
their future experiences.

00:38:02.920 --> 00:38:05.320
In this case, I
have the same day,

00:38:05.320 --> 00:38:07.960
same time that three
different customers who

00:38:07.960 --> 00:38:10.060
came to the Zulily site.

00:38:10.060 --> 00:38:13.090
On your left, extreme
left, is a customer who

00:38:13.090 --> 00:38:17.590
buys apparel and fitness gear.

00:38:17.590 --> 00:38:19.750
In the middle was
a shopper who came

00:38:19.750 --> 00:38:25.210
through footwear advertisement
and landed on our site.

00:38:25.210 --> 00:38:27.430
And on the extreme
right is a customer

00:38:27.430 --> 00:38:31.490
who predominantly buys for
her kids and home goods.

00:38:31.490 --> 00:38:36.460
So you can see the experience
is radically different for all

00:38:36.460 --> 00:38:38.530
of our customers.

00:38:38.530 --> 00:38:41.690
And this is key to
driving engagement.

00:38:41.690 --> 00:38:45.820
This goes back to by
doing all of that,

00:38:45.820 --> 00:38:47.590
we were able to get to--

00:38:47.590 --> 00:38:49.930
91% of our orders
today are actually

00:38:49.930 --> 00:38:52.300
coming from repeat customers.

00:38:52.300 --> 00:38:57.220
We have over 6.1 million
active customers today.

00:38:57.220 --> 00:39:02.450
Our number of active customers
has grown 24% year over year.

00:39:02.450 --> 00:39:08.050
Our revenues at the end of Q1
had grown 17% year over year.

00:39:08.050 --> 00:39:09.850
Currently, we are
at $1.6 billion.

00:39:13.330 --> 00:39:15.700
And one of the things
I wanted to mention--

00:39:15.700 --> 00:39:20.080
I mentioned this in the context
of the data platform itself--

00:39:20.080 --> 00:39:25.240
is the data lives in
Google Cloud Storage.

00:39:25.240 --> 00:39:27.470
We are gathering
all of the data.

00:39:27.470 --> 00:39:30.910
But even to power the machine
learning and personalization

00:39:30.910 --> 00:39:36.220
experience, we are actually
leveraging the Hadoop Spark

00:39:36.220 --> 00:39:40.060
ecosystem, the Dataproc clusters
that Google has to offer.

00:39:40.060 --> 00:39:42.640
And using that, we
are creating all the

00:39:42.640 --> 00:39:47.680
features that we need to drive
the personalization experience.

00:39:47.680 --> 00:39:49.780
And again, depending on
what kind of features

00:39:49.780 --> 00:39:51.850
we are building, what
kind of transformations

00:39:51.850 --> 00:39:52.900
the cadence will vary.

00:39:52.900 --> 00:39:54.190
Some are near real-time.

00:39:54.190 --> 00:39:58.446
Some are every half hour,
every day, and so forth.

00:39:58.446 --> 00:40:00.070
One of the other
interesting challenges

00:40:00.070 --> 00:40:05.500
here is, people who weren't
familiar with the machine

00:40:05.500 --> 00:40:08.470
learning personalisation
recommendation systems

00:40:08.470 --> 00:40:16.950
space is, there is an inherent
presentation bias in machine

00:40:16.950 --> 00:40:18.060
learning jargon.

00:40:18.060 --> 00:40:19.390
And what does that mean?

00:40:19.390 --> 00:40:22.992
That means, if a customer
never sees a product,

00:40:22.992 --> 00:40:24.450
they're never going
to click on it.

00:40:24.450 --> 00:40:28.110
If they're never clicking to it,
they're never going to buy it.

00:40:28.110 --> 00:40:32.850
So one of the places where
we use Google Cloud Storage

00:40:32.850 --> 00:40:36.130
was also to address that
problem of presentation bias.

00:40:36.130 --> 00:40:39.510
How do we bring transparency
into what we do and how we do,

00:40:39.510 --> 00:40:41.820
and share it with
our business partners

00:40:41.820 --> 00:40:45.210
so they understand what is the
exposure that personalization

00:40:45.210 --> 00:40:48.330
is driving through
the customer base.

00:40:48.330 --> 00:40:51.480
And we did that by
having all of our logs.

00:40:51.480 --> 00:40:53.220
So when we are serving--

00:40:53.220 --> 00:40:58.410
by the way, these are
served in near real-time.

00:40:58.410 --> 00:41:00.960
So as we are serving
these experiences

00:41:00.960 --> 00:41:04.210
to customers based on their
latest and greatest data,

00:41:04.210 --> 00:41:06.270
we are actually logging
those experiences.

00:41:06.270 --> 00:41:09.390
And we make those
logs available in GCS,

00:41:09.390 --> 00:41:13.610
which helps us to actually mine
those logs after the fact--

00:41:13.610 --> 00:41:16.740
or during the course of the
day at periodic intervals--

00:41:16.740 --> 00:41:19.800
and be able to present
reports and dashboards to all

00:41:19.800 --> 00:41:24.810
of our partners, telling them
how personalization is actually

00:41:24.810 --> 00:41:28.230
driving exposure from our
customers on the products

00:41:28.230 --> 00:41:31.740
that they cared about which
was very insightful as well.

00:41:35.470 --> 00:41:37.710
So I mentioned
briefly how it works.

00:41:37.710 --> 00:41:39.780
We are launching millions
of sites every day.

00:41:39.780 --> 00:41:41.370
We receive all of that data.

00:41:41.370 --> 00:41:42.730
We process that data.

00:41:42.730 --> 00:41:46.890
This is where Google Cloud
Storage comes into the picture.

00:41:46.890 --> 00:41:51.690
We are using Dataproc cluster
to train on machine learning

00:41:51.690 --> 00:41:53.430
models based on the
features that we

00:41:53.430 --> 00:41:55.920
are deriving from that data.

00:41:55.920 --> 00:41:59.070
And we are using that to
inform the future experiences

00:41:59.070 --> 00:42:00.660
for our customers .

00:42:00.660 --> 00:42:02.790
It drives higher
revenue engagement.

00:42:02.790 --> 00:42:05.170
But above all, it makes
our customers happy.

00:42:10.220 --> 00:42:16.890
So there are other places where
my partners the marketing side

00:42:16.890 --> 00:42:20.830
are actually using that data
to actually figure out what

00:42:20.830 --> 00:42:22.780
the customer lifetime value is.

00:42:22.780 --> 00:42:25.570
And they do that by really
looking at that data

00:42:25.570 --> 00:42:30.040
again and seeing, OK, who
are our existing high-value

00:42:30.040 --> 00:42:31.270
customers?

00:42:31.270 --> 00:42:33.490
What were the
behaviors that actually

00:42:33.490 --> 00:42:35.540
separate these from the rest?

00:42:35.540 --> 00:42:38.500
And as we are trying to
acquire new customers,

00:42:38.500 --> 00:42:41.710
can we actually find who
those high-value customers

00:42:41.710 --> 00:42:44.780
are based on the behaviors
that they are exhibiting?

00:42:44.780 --> 00:42:48.790
So again, it unlocks,
unleashes a lot of power,

00:42:48.790 --> 00:42:51.655
a lot of capability that
you can build on top.

00:42:56.280 --> 00:42:57.930
So it's been a great journey.

00:42:57.930 --> 00:42:59.960
I talked about all
of the positives.

00:42:59.960 --> 00:43:03.290
I do want to talk about what
are some of the key challenges

00:43:03.290 --> 00:43:04.920
as well.

00:43:04.920 --> 00:43:08.510
One of the things that James
mentioned I want to echo--

00:43:08.510 --> 00:43:12.200
which was a positive,
and I missed it--

00:43:12.200 --> 00:43:14.510
was the strong consistency.

00:43:14.510 --> 00:43:17.360
We are actually gathering
this data at scale.

00:43:17.360 --> 00:43:18.530
It's really high volume.

00:43:18.530 --> 00:43:21.500
We're looking at billions
of key data points

00:43:21.500 --> 00:43:23.520
over the course of the day.

00:43:23.520 --> 00:43:26.930
So we want to make sure as one
process is writing the data,

00:43:26.930 --> 00:43:29.180
while the other process
is reading that data,

00:43:29.180 --> 00:43:31.460
it has the latest
and greatest data.

00:43:31.460 --> 00:43:34.640
So the imaged consistency
in Google Cloud Storage

00:43:34.640 --> 00:43:37.625
is critical to enable that.

00:43:37.625 --> 00:43:38.750
So what are the challenges?

00:43:38.750 --> 00:43:43.670
And what are things that
would be great to have?

00:43:43.670 --> 00:43:46.250
I mentioned at the
beginning of my talk

00:43:46.250 --> 00:43:49.310
that this is more on repository
for analytics and machine

00:43:49.310 --> 00:43:50.390
learning.

00:43:50.390 --> 00:43:55.280
If it's a repository, it has to
have some mechanism of making

00:43:55.280 --> 00:43:57.920
that data discoverable.

00:43:57.920 --> 00:44:00.690
Currently, that is one of
the challenges we deal with.

00:44:00.690 --> 00:44:04.350
We're the different teams
generating different data sets.

00:44:04.350 --> 00:44:07.100
How do we make sure that
we are able to re-use

00:44:07.100 --> 00:44:09.340
some of the capability a
different team has already

00:44:09.340 --> 00:44:10.280
produced?

00:44:10.280 --> 00:44:13.010
How do we enable that
ease of discovery

00:44:13.010 --> 00:44:15.050
in the Google Cloud Storage?

00:44:15.050 --> 00:44:18.760
One of the things I've loved
working with the Google team

00:44:18.760 --> 00:44:24.230
is the collaboration,
that we work together

00:44:24.230 --> 00:44:27.170
to identify such problems
and make improvements

00:44:27.170 --> 00:44:29.490
on the products.

00:44:29.490 --> 00:44:31.730
So that is a conversation
we are having.

00:44:31.730 --> 00:44:35.300
I don't want to steal
any thunder from them.

00:44:35.300 --> 00:44:38.630
But this is something that's
critical to me personally.

00:44:38.630 --> 00:44:42.950
And I recognize that it is for
other partners, other companies

00:44:42.950 --> 00:44:43.950
as well.

00:44:43.950 --> 00:44:47.165
The other is regulations.

00:44:47.165 --> 00:44:48.570
So we are living
in a world where

00:44:48.570 --> 00:44:49.920
regulations are revolving.

00:44:49.920 --> 00:44:51.440
They're compliance.

00:44:51.440 --> 00:44:54.980
So how do we ensure
that all of these data

00:44:54.980 --> 00:44:59.430
sets that we've
accrued over time is--

00:44:59.430 --> 00:45:05.510
how easy is it to incorporate a
new regulation, new compliance,

00:45:05.510 --> 00:45:07.060
or legal clause?

00:45:07.060 --> 00:45:10.310
That's one of the challenges
where we had to rethink--

00:45:10.310 --> 00:45:14.130
again, we are a
relatively young company,

00:45:14.130 --> 00:45:16.310
just been there eight years.

00:45:16.310 --> 00:45:19.640
We never had to deal
with decades-old data.

00:45:19.640 --> 00:45:22.790
But that's something that we've
started thinking about now,

00:45:22.790 --> 00:45:25.610
is what is that data
retention policy?

00:45:25.610 --> 00:45:28.040
And how does that play
into Google Cloud Storage?

00:45:28.040 --> 00:45:32.240
So for people who are actually
on-boarding Google Now,

00:45:32.240 --> 00:45:34.740
I think that would be one of
the things you should just

00:45:34.740 --> 00:45:37.790
bake in and think
about now versus later.

00:45:40.570 --> 00:45:44.560
Data annotation, duplication
ties into data discovery.

00:45:44.560 --> 00:45:46.740
But this is, again,
more on duplication.

00:45:46.740 --> 00:45:50.980
I'll be repeating the
work, and we actually

00:45:50.980 --> 00:45:53.620
take advantage of the work
that somebody else has

00:45:53.620 --> 00:45:58.160
done to generate a data
set and use that instead.

00:45:58.160 --> 00:46:00.430
So those are the
things that I think

00:46:00.430 --> 00:46:01.930
are really top of mind for me.

00:46:01.930 --> 00:46:03.870
And I would love to solve.

00:46:03.870 --> 00:46:08.650
And I would love to solve it
in collaboration with Google.

00:46:08.650 --> 00:46:13.430
So I will pass on
the mic to James.

00:46:13.430 --> 00:46:17.694
And we will be open
to questions in a bit.

00:46:17.694 --> 00:46:19.110
JAMES MALONE: Thank
you very much.

00:46:23.130 --> 00:46:25.420
All right, so before we
leave and take questions--

00:46:25.420 --> 00:46:27.510
we may have a few minutes
for questions here--

00:46:27.510 --> 00:46:29.760
I wanted to leave you
with a few resources

00:46:29.760 --> 00:46:32.010
that you could look at,
that you could get involved

00:46:32.010 --> 00:46:33.900
with for more information.

00:46:33.900 --> 00:46:37.860
If this was interesting, if this
topic is near and dear to you,

00:46:37.860 --> 00:46:40.630
we wanted to point you
in the right direction.

00:46:40.630 --> 00:46:41.820
So there is a talk.

00:46:41.820 --> 00:46:42.590
It is happening.

00:46:42.590 --> 00:46:44.610
And it's actually
after this talk.

00:46:44.610 --> 00:46:47.890
It's an IO talk on
moving data to the cloud.

00:46:47.890 --> 00:46:50.400
It's going to cover much more
specifically how you actually

00:46:50.400 --> 00:46:53.880
do something like move large
sets of data to cloud storage.

00:46:53.880 --> 00:46:56.950
If that topic is interesting
to you, please do check it out.

00:46:56.950 --> 00:46:58.500
There's also a lot
of documentation

00:46:58.500 --> 00:47:02.400
on the cloud.google.com site
for building a data lake,

00:47:02.400 --> 00:47:06.480
thinking about the lifecycle of
data on Google Cloud Platform,

00:47:06.480 --> 00:47:09.060
and using data in
cloud storage as a data

00:47:09.060 --> 00:47:11.430
lake with our big data tools.

00:47:11.430 --> 00:47:14.580
You also find that a lot of
the products like Dataproc,

00:47:14.580 --> 00:47:18.270
or Dataflow, BigQuery actually
have their own documentation as

00:47:18.270 --> 00:47:20.310
well for working
with cloud storage

00:47:20.310 --> 00:47:22.552
for usually more
specific use cases,

00:47:22.552 --> 00:47:24.510
whether it'd be something
like Spark Streaming,

00:47:24.510 --> 00:47:26.760
or streaming an Apache
Beam, or doing a Federated

00:47:26.760 --> 00:47:28.350
query in BigQuery.

00:47:28.350 --> 00:47:31.500
So if you are curious on how
to use cloud storage as a data

00:47:31.500 --> 00:47:35.197
lake with product x,y, or z,
I would actually recommend--

00:47:35.197 --> 00:47:37.530
look at the documentation for
that product, and usually,

00:47:37.530 --> 00:47:38.400
you will find that--

00:47:38.400 --> 00:47:42.950
[MUSIC PLAYING]

